---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="celsius-to-kelvin">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="celsius-to-kelvin" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Same degree, different zero</h2>
    <p>
      Kelvin and Celsius are the only pair of temperature scales with the same degree size. One kelvin
      is exactly one degree Celsius; only the position of zero differs. The conversion is one addition:
    </p>
    <p><strong>K = &deg;C + 273.15</strong></p>
    <p>
      There is no multiplication and no fraction. 0 &deg;C is 273.15 K, 25 &deg;C is 298.15 K, and
      100 &deg;C is 373.15 K.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 273.15 and not 273</h2>
    <p>
      The kelvin was first defined by putting the triple point of water (the one temperature and
      pressure at which ice, liquid water and vapour coexist) at exactly 273.16 K. The ordinary ice
      point is 0.01 K below that, at 273.15 K, and Celsius calls that point zero. Since the 2019 SI
      redefinition, the Boltzmann constant fixes the kelvin, but the numbers were chosen so that
      nothing changed in practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can drop the 0.15 in a school exercise but not in thermodynamics, where absolute temperatures
      appear in ratios and a 0.05% error propagates.
    </p>

    <h2>Writing it correctly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Write <strong>300 K</strong>, not 300 &deg;K. Kelvin is not a degree scale.</li>
      <li>Say "three hundred kelvin", not "three hundred degrees kelvin".</li>
      <li>The unit symbol is a capital K; the unit name, when written out, is lower case.</li>
      <li>You can give temperature differences in K or &deg;C, because the degree size is the same.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reference points on the absolute scale</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kelvin</th><th scope="col">Celsius</th><th scope="col">What happens there</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>-273.15</td><td>Absolute zero: you can approach it but not reach it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2.7</td><td>-270.45</td><td>The cosmic microwave background</td></tr>
        <tr><td>77</td><td>-196.15</td><td>Liquid nitrogen boils</td></tr>
        <tr><td>273.15</td><td>0</td><td>Ice melts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>293.15</td><td>20</td><td>Room temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>310.15</td><td>37</td><td>Human body temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>373.15</td><td>100</td><td>Water boils at sea level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1811</td><td>1537.85</td><td>Iron melts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5772</td><td>5498.85</td><td>The Sun&apos;s photosphere</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Where kelvin is compulsory</h2>
    <p>
      Use kelvin when temperature appears in a physical law as a ratio or a product: the ideal gas law,
      the Stefan&ndash;Boltzmann law, blackbody spectra, reaction-rate equations, noise figures in
      electronics. Two times the absolute temperature of a gas at constant pressure gives two times
      the volume. Two times the Celsius temperature gives no predictable result, because the Celsius
      zero is arbitrary.
    </p>
    <p>
      You also see kelvin in lighting: a "2700 K" bulb is warm white and a "6500 K" bulb is daylight.
      This is a colour temperature (the temperature of a blackbody that radiates that colour), not the
      temperature of the bulb.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
